# Ava — CUDA profile. Ollama with NVIDIA GPU acceleration, serving quantized
# GGUF weights. The middle rung between `gpu` (vLLM, FP16) and `cpu`.
#
# deploy/install.sh selects this for an NVIDIA box whose card is too small for
# vLLM but big enough to hold a quantized model on-device. vLLM's floor is
# ~18000 MiB for the shipped 7B (14.2 GiB of FP16 weights plus a 32k KV cache at
# 0.90 utilization) and ~12000 MiB for the 3B it downshifts to. Ollama's is a
# different sum entirely, because the weights are Q4 rather than FP16:
#
#   llama3.2 (3B) Q4_K_M weights                            2.0 GiB
#   KV cache, 8192 tokens, fp16
#     (28 layers x 8 KV heads x 128 dim x 2 x 2 B
#      = 0.109 MiB/token, x 8192 tokens)                    0.9 GiB
#   CUDA context + compute/graph buffers                    0.6 GiB
#   framebuffer a laptop card already spends driving
#     the screen it is attached to                          0.5 GiB
#   ---------------------------------------------------------------
#   weights + KV + context + headroom                       4.0 GiB = 4096 MiB
#
# So: under 4096 MiB install.sh picks `cpu`, and at 12000 MiB or more it picks
# `gpu`. Everything between is this profile — which is where an RTX A1000 6GB,
# an RTX 3050 and every other 6-8 GB laptop card lives. They used to be handed
# CPU-only Ollama, several times slower, because the installer applied vLLM's
# FP16 floor to a decision that lands on Ollama.
#
#   cd deploy && cp profiles/cuda.env .env && docker compose up -d
#
# FIRST RUN pulls the model (install.sh does this for you):
#   docker compose exec ollama-cuda ollama pull llama3.2
#
# REQUIREMENTS on the host: the NVIDIA Container Toolkit, not merely the driver.
# `nvidia-smi` working proves the host can talk to the card; it proves nothing
# about the daemon being able to hand it to a container. Check with:
#   docker run --rm --gpus all nvidia/cuda:12.4.0-base-ubuntu22.04 nvidia-smi
# install.sh asks the same question (`docker info` -> is an `nvidia` runtime
# registered) and falls back to `cpu` rather than starting a service the daemon
# will refuse. Override that check with AVA_SKIP_GPU_RUNTIME_CHECK=1.
#
# ROOM TO GROW: on ~8 GB or more an 8B Q4_K_M (~4.7 GiB of weights) also fits.
# Ollama tags, not Hugging Face repo ids, on this profile:
#   AVA_MODEL=llama3.1:8b ./install.sh
# Ollama also degrades gracefully where vLLM does not — a model slightly too
# large is split across GPU and CPU rather than failing to load — so trying a
# bigger tag costs a slower turn, not a restart loop.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=cuda

AVA_BACKEND_URL=http://ollama-cuda:11434/v1
AVA_BACKEND_ENGINE=ollama
# REQUIRED — Ava ships no default model. Any Ollama tag you have pulled.
AVA_MODEL=
